%I A400096 #26 Nov 26 2026 17:48:29
%S A400096 1,2,4,5,8,10,11,16,17,20,22,23,25,32,34,40,41,44,46,47,50,55,61,64,
%T A400096 67,68,80,82,83,85,88,89,92,94,100,101,103,110,115,121,122,125,128,
%U A400096 134,136,137,151,160,164,166,167,170,176,178,179,184,187,188,193,200,202
%N A400096 Numbers k such that the trajectory of k->ord(3,k) reaches 1 before reaching a number not coprime to 3. Here ord(b,k) is the multiplicative order of b modulo k.
%C A400096 For given b in general, let S(b) be the set of k such that the trajectory of k->ord(b,k) reaches 1 before reaching a number not coprime to b. Then:
%C A400096   (i) If k is in S(b), then every divisor of k is in S(b);
%C A400096   (ii) As lcm(b^x - 1, b^y - 1) divides b^lcm(x,y) - 1, by induction the LCM of every pair of elements in S(b) is in S(b);
%C A400096   (iii) As ord(b,p^r) divides p^(r-1)*ord(b,p), the powers of a prime p in S(b) are also in S(b).
%C A400096 By these properties S(b) is closed under multiplication, in fact a monoid generated by its prime members.
%C A400096 It is easy to show that S(b) is contained in S(b^e) for e >= 1. The followings are equivalent:
%C A400096   (i) e is in S(b);
%C A400096   (ii) e is in S(b^e);
%C A400096   (iii) S(b^e) = S(b).
%C A400096 Proof: (i) => (ii) is obvious.
%C A400096   (ii) => (iii). Suppose k > 1 is in S(b^e), and every element smaller than k in S(b^e) is in S(b). Then ord(b^e,k) is in S(b^e), and so ord(b,k) (which divides e*ord(b^e,k)) is in S(b^e). By induction ord(b,k) is in S(b), which implies that k is in S(b).
%C A400096   (iii) => (i). By (iii) b^e - 1 is in S(b), so e is in S(b).
%C A400096 Since ord(b,k)/ord(-b,k) is in {1/2, 1, 2}, we have S(b) = S(-b) for all odd b.
%C A400096 By the two properties above, this sequence is also numbers k such that the trajectory of k->ord(b,k) reaches 1 before reaching a number not coprime to b, for b = 9, -3, -9, ...
%H A400096 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400096/b400096.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A400096 Powers of 2 are terms: 2^n -> 2^(n-2) -> 2^(n-4) -> ... -> 8 or 4 -> 2 -> 1;
%e A400096 11 is a term: 11 -> 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1.
%t A400096 okQ[k_, b_:3] := NestWhile[MultiplicativeOrder[b, #] &, k, # > 1 && CoprimeQ[b, #] &] == 1;
%t A400096 Select[Range[200], okQ] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 26 2026 *)
%o A400096 (PARI) isok(n,b=3) = while(gcd(n,b)==1, n=znorder(Mod(b,n)); if(n==1, return(1))); return(0)
%Y A400096 Sequences for bases b: this sequence (b=+-3 or +-9), A400097 (b=4), A400098 (b=+-5), A400099 (b=6), A400100 (b=+-7), A400331 (b=8), A400442 (b=10), A400462 (b=-2 or -8), A400866 (b=-4), A400976 (b=-6), A401428 (b=-10).
%K A400096 nonn,new
%O A400096 1,2
%A A400096 _Jianing Song_, Nov 24 2026
